well let's get labour's view on this now  shall we i'm joined by the shadow  business secretary jonathan reynolds a  very good morning  so the economy contracting in in the  second quarter we had that news this  morning meanwhile we're warned that  energy bills could go up as high as 5  000 pounds  next year so what exactly is labour's  plan of how it would be tackled well  good morning yes i mean unfortunately it  seems there's a crisis in every aspect  of national life under this government  at the moment but there's no doubt that  the energy emergency is the most  significant and pressing one facing  households and businesses now we've been  setting out since january of this year  that plan for a windfall tax of using  that revenue to support households there  is already a win  well it doesn't it begins actually we  would go back to january when we made  that case when you started to see that  windfall occurring so we have more  revenue that we could put into the  system but obviously as the crisis has  only got worse since we began making the  case for that clearly further action is  required now we're going to be sending  that out over the weeks and days ahead  but i can tell you today that part of  that response will be for instance we  will end uh the immoral practice of  people who pay for their energy in  advance through a prepayment meter  that's about four million households in  the uk being charged more than people  who pay by direct debits so we would  stop there being a higher cap for those  pre-payment customers we'd give them the  relief now we'd be able to reimburse  energy suppliers for the cost of that  running into march next year because we  can raise more revenue through the  windfall tax to clear targeted tax that  we put forward but we're going to  continue to do this there is no doubt we  don't need any more information about  the scale of the crisis we get it we  understand it we're going to be making  the case for that well if you get it and  you understand it why are we just  getting an announcement on pre-payment  today i mean we've had lots of ideas  from the liberal democrats had lots of  ideas from gordon brown as well so why  isn't labour telling us exactly what the  plan is well i would say we go back to  january of this year when we started no  but you don't have a clear plan  now we do have a very clear plan now  what we what we're saying today is we  recognize this crisis has only got worse  and we're going to continue with our  response to match the the scale of that  now why the windfall tax argument from  us i think was successful was because  not only we were calling for action but  we had a very clear plan as to how we'd  spend the revenue of that where help  would go to what people could expect so  we're going to make sure we have the  detail of that and we have that for next  week but we're making the case that we  get the scale of the crisis we've been  calling for action since january but  we're going to continue to do so and  each time we're going to make sure  people recognize the specific measures  that would benefit them and in this case  today's those prepayment customers four  million households who get extra support  okay so we have to wait for more details  but what about general theories let's  ask you about what you made of what  gordon brown has said this week he  talked about uh for energy companies  that don't  offer lower bills aren't able to charge  people less that they should be  temporarily re-nationalized now is that  a good idea well i very much welcome  what gordon brown said this week because  he's recognizing the scale of the crisis  i also welcome by the way as a reminder  that we have had in the past prime  ministers labor prime ministers who can  deal with the crisis in this case the  global financial crisis and respond to  that now i would just say on the case  that gordon made he was right to say  that the state has to act as the supply  of last resort to make sure people can  get gas and electric if their supplier  goes out or business that's already  broadly the case in terms of how the  system works but where we need specific  action is in you you can take supply  companies into public ownership but  that's not going to solve the problem  the windfall is accruing on the  production side and therefore that's  right the windfall tax is the right  intervention to take a share of that  windfall for the benefit of the economy  as a whole and that's the right thing to  do so i think it's very important we  understand we get across to people  that's the action that is required in  itself bringing a public company into  public control isn't a response to the  crisis but he's right to say the state  has to guarantee the supply then okay  but let me be clear is labor against  nationalization well it's not part of  our response to the cost of living  because what is required there is direct  financial support for people which we  would raise through a windfall tax it's  just important to say obviously that if  you look at where the country has has  been coming through the pandemic  borrowing a lot of money that was  absolutely the right thing to do but  over time the share of debt to the size  of the economy does have to shrink and  there are limitations that come from the  fact that you've had to increase public  borrowing in response to the pandemic  you said you welcomed an intervention  from the likes of gordon brown who you  say is greatly respected so let's take a  look at something else that he said  yesterday time and tide wait for no one  he said neither do crises they don't  take holidays and don't politely hang  fire was that a dig at sakisama who's  been on holiday no not at all i think  that was not about i think what gordon  brown is pointing out is we have this  incredible crisis the scale of which has  real consequences for people and we have  no effective functioning government i  mean there is simply no response to that  akira is actually in scotland today he's  doing some media doing some live  interviews around a few things including  the response to this no i don't think  that was a dig at all i think what  gordon was right to say in the past and  he's a good example of this we've seen  governments labor governments respond to  challenges that are very significant why  can't we have a response from this  government to this crisis but kirsten  wasn't around this week there is no plan  forthcoming yet so having a dig at the  government would be missing an action  it's not right to say there's no plan  yet i mean go back we haven't got the  full well the windfall tax plans we're  moving vat off energy bills it's  removing some of the levees that appear  on people's bills and changing how they  are paid for massively increasing the  scope of warmth homes discount in terms  of the value of that to people and who  is eligible for that so about a third of  british households would get that going  further today by saying people who use  prepayment meters won't have that  additional cost and we're going to  continue do so so i think we are putting  forward that plan but the fact is every  day the crisis is getting worse so the  response has to grow to that okay so uh  more detail to come all right jonathan  reynolds thanks very much thank you
 

